WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:04.440
This video is a first walk through UM-Codex on Windows.

00:04.440 --> 00:06.800
You'll open its window, make a setup,

00:06.800 --> 00:10.160
and see every choice in it, including where Codex runs.

00:10.160 --> 00:15.224
Then you'll start Codex, ask Codex to do something, and stop and start again.

00:16.424 --> 00:20.844
Open UM-Codex from your Desktop, and its window opens in your browser.

00:20.844 --> 00:24.244
It's a page running on your own computer, not a website.

00:24.244 --> 00:27.864
The line at the top says Docker is running and your key is saved.

00:27.864 --> 00:31.924
Under it is Get started, a short list: your key is saved,

00:31.924 --> 00:35.424
Docker is running, and the last step is Make your first setup.

00:35.424 --> 00:39.800
A setup is a folder, plus what Codex may reach and where it runs.

00:41.000 --> 00:42.300
Press New setup.

00:42.300 --> 00:44.040
The first part is Folder.

00:44.040 --> 00:46.480
The working folder is where Codex starts.

00:46.480 --> 00:49.420
It can read, change and delete files there.

00:49.420 --> 00:52.260
They're your real files, with no undo.

00:52.260 --> 00:54.540
Under it, you can add more folders,

00:54.540 --> 00:58.616
and say for each one whether Codex can only read it, or read and write.

00:59.116 --> 01:01.136
Access has the internet switch.

01:01.136 --> 01:03.956
With it on, Codex can reach the whole internet,

01:03.956 --> 01:06.036
so it could send what it can read anywhere,

01:06.036 --> 01:09.316
including programs on your computer and your local network.

01:09.316 --> 01:12.276
With it off, Codex can reach only the model.

01:12.276 --> 01:14.116
Under it is the browser tool,

01:14.116 --> 01:19.228
which gives Codex a fresh browser inside the sealed environment, with none of your logins.

01:19.728 --> 01:20.908
Then Codex.

01:20.908 --> 01:22.948
Pick the model, newest first.

01:22.948 --> 01:26.128
And pick where Codex opens: in a Terminal window,

01:26.128 --> 01:28.788
or in the Codex app, which is the default.

01:28.788 --> 01:33.468
The Codex app here is UM-Codex's own copy of it, with its own settings.

01:33.468 --> 01:36.288
Your own ChatGPT app isn't touched.

01:36.788 --> 01:40.488
Under More options, Ask before commands is off by default.

01:40.488 --> 01:45.768
Codex then runs commands without asking, and the sealed environment is what keeps it in.

01:45.768 --> 01:48.620
Turn it on if you want to approve each command yourself.

01:49.120 --> 01:51.740
The most important choice is Where Codex runs.

01:51.740 --> 01:54.000
The first choice is In the sandbox,

01:54.000 --> 01:58.380
which is what the screen calls the sealed environment, and it's the recommended one.

01:58.380 --> 02:01.520
Codex works in a sealed environment on your computer.

02:01.520 --> 02:03.720
It sees only the folders you gave it.

02:03.720 --> 02:05.920
Your Toolkit key stays outside it:

02:05.920 --> 02:09.740
a small relay on your computer adds the key to Codex's requests,

02:09.740 --> 02:12.112
so nothing Codex runs can read it.

02:12.612 --> 02:16.392
The second choice is On this computer, and it's experimental.

02:16.392 --> 02:20.692
Codex then runs on the computer itself, not in the sealed environment.

02:20.692 --> 02:22.512
It can do anything you can do.

02:22.512 --> 02:25.552
It can read, change and delete any of your files,

02:25.552 --> 02:28.912
use your apps and your browser, and act with your accounts.

02:28.912 --> 02:33.612
It might even be able to reach UM-Codex's own key, because it runs as you.

02:33.612 --> 02:37.668
Use it only when you need Codex to control the computer or the browser.

02:38.168 --> 02:41.288
On Windows, that choice is greyed out for now.

02:41.288 --> 02:45.688
The line under it says it works with the Codex app on a Mac only, for now.

02:45.688 --> 02:48.896
So everything in this video runs in the sealed environment.

02:49.396 --> 02:53.316
At the bottom, What Codex gets says it all in plain words:

02:53.316 --> 02:56.496
the folder, the access, and the Codex settings.

02:56.496 --> 02:58.416
It ends the same way every time:

02:58.416 --> 03:02.776
your Toolkit key stays on this computer, and the sandbox never sees it.

03:02.776 --> 03:06.056
Save and start saves the setup and opens Codex.

03:06.056 --> 03:07.576
Save only saves it.

03:07.576 --> 03:09.484
And Cancel throws it away.

03:10.684 --> 03:14.804
Press Choose folder, and pick a folder in Windows' own folder dialog.

03:14.804 --> 03:18.204
For this video, it's a demo folder of made-up files.

03:18.204 --> 03:23.324
For anything important, work in a copy, or in a folder that's a git repository.

03:23.324 --> 03:25.324
Then press Save and start.

03:25.824 --> 03:27.044
That's all you do.

03:27.044 --> 03:30.324
The folder is now a setup, a card named after it.

03:30.324 --> 03:34.248
Its line goes from Starting, to Opening Codex, to Connected.

03:34.748 --> 03:37.828
UM-Codex opens its own copy of the Codex app,

03:37.828 --> 03:40.568
in a window of its own, with its own settings.

03:40.568 --> 03:43.288
It opens on a project named after your folder.

03:43.288 --> 03:48.068
At the bottom, it says Remote, and the sandbox's name, with a green dot.

03:48.068 --> 03:51.148
A chat that says Remote runs in the sealed environment.

03:51.148 --> 03:55.700
A chat that doesn't would run on your computer itself, so UM-Codex blocks it.

03:56.200 --> 03:59.540
You may see the app's own banner, Full access is on.

03:59.540 --> 04:01.000
That's the app's wording.

04:01.000 --> 04:06.352
Inside UM-Codex it applies only within the sealed environment and the folders you chose.

04:06.852 --> 04:09.292
Now ask Codex for something in the folder.

04:09.292 --> 04:13.492
Here, it summarizes the spreadsheet and writes a short note about it.

04:13.492 --> 04:18.292
Codex works inside the sealed environment, and the note lands in your real folder.

04:18.292 --> 04:21.156
View changes shows exactly what it changed.

04:21.656 --> 04:25.296
Back in the window, the card sums the setup up in one line:

04:25.296 --> 04:28.536
where Codex opens, the internet, and the model.

04:28.536 --> 04:33.008
Open What Codex can do here for the full wording, the same as in the form.

04:34.208 --> 04:36.148
When you're finished, press Stop.

04:36.148 --> 04:37.428
It asks first.

04:37.428 --> 04:40.268
Codex stops and its sandbox is removed.

04:40.268 --> 04:45.108
Files it already changed in your folder stay as they are, and its history is kept.

04:45.108 --> 04:47.948
The Codex app then says it can't reconnect.

04:47.948 --> 04:49.280
That's expected.

04:49.780 --> 04:52.620
Next time, Start is at the top of the card.

04:52.620 --> 04:53.620
One click.

04:53.620 --> 04:57.500
Under the card are four links: Edit, to change the setup,

04:57.500 --> 04:59.960
Rename, Duplicate, and Delete, which asks first.

04:59.960 --> 05:06.052
At the top of the window, Check for updates and Replace your key are always there.

05:06.552 --> 05:08.012
That's your first launch.

05:08.012 --> 05:12.240
Next, what each setting means, and the same window on a Mac.
